--- A function handed as a value to a function that applies it (`apply_to[double, 5]`),
--- and then an item of a list of functions handed to the same function
--- (`apply_to[fs[0], 5]`): an internal error in both modes.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i) and compiled (crow run) alike, exit 1, nothing printed:
---     demings_funnel-1.co: internal error: the lowered program disagrees with itself — a bug
---     in Crow, not in this program: demings_funnel-1 main: line 27: hands 'apply_to' Fns(double) for
---     its parameter 'f' (argument 1), which is Fn(Int) -> Int
--- Expected:
---     10
---     10
--- The first call types apply_to's `f` as Fn(Int) -> Int; the second hands it a group
--- (Fns) and nothing joins the two. The same with a group of two functions, each
--- also handed alone (`[double, triple]`). Found writing Deming's funnel: four rule
--- functions handed one at a time to `funnel`, and then from a list `[rule1, …]`.
--- Without the first call the program is refused instead ("double's parameter 'x' is
--- typed by nothing", findings/call_a_function-1.co).

fn double[x]
    x * 2

fn apply_to[f, v]
    f[v]

log[apply_to[double, 5]]
fs = [double]
log[apply_to[fs[0], 5]]
